{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Per-class fallback chains\n\n## Context / Problem\nA class may name a model that is not always reachable — a local endpoint that is down, a provider\nbeing throttled. Today a single unreachable model fails the gate outright; there is no way to say\n\"use the local trivial model, but fall back to a hosted one\".\n\n## Approach\nConsume the `fallback` array that the schema ticket already parses, constructing pydantic-ai's\n`FallbackModel(default, *fallbacks, fallback_on=...)` per class. Fallback entries are INLINE model\nconfigs (same shape as a slot), not references to named presets, so nothing requires the operator to\nlook up rebar vocabulary.\n\n`fallback_on` must be set deliberately rather than left at its default. pydantic-ai's own docs warn\nthat provider SDK retry logic can delay failover substantially — a 429 may be retried inside the SDK\nfor up to ~60s before the fallback is ever reached — so the condition is narrowed to the errors where\nswitching providers actually helps (availability/rate-limit classes), not every exception.\n\n**Alternative rejected — rebar-implemented retry/failover.** pydantic-ai already ships this and rebar\nalready depends on it; a second implementation would diverge from the runner's error taxonomy.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/` — construct `FallbackModel` from a class's fallback list where the model is built.\n- `tests/unit/` — construction and selection tests.\n\n## Testing\nUnit tier with a stubbed model: no network, no live provider.\n- a class with no `fallback` builds the plain model (no FallbackModel wrapper) — today's behaviour.\n- a class with one fallback builds a FallbackModel whose default is the primary.\n- an error in the configured `fallback_on` class selects the fallback.\n- an error OUTSIDE that class propagates instead of silently failing over.\n\n## DECIDED — the five runtime questions, against the VERIFIED pydantic-ai API\n\nThe first revision of this section asserted mechanisms without checking pydantic-ai's actual surface,\nand the review correctly blocked all three. Every claim below now cites the installed source at\n`.venv/.../pydantic_ai/models/fallback.py` and `.../messages.py`.\n\n**1. `endpoint` on a fallback entry IS honored.** `resolve_fallback_chain` returns `provider:model`\nSTRINGS, which structurally cannot carry an endpoint, so it is NOT the construction input — it stays\nas the human-readable / provenance view. Construction consumes the full `FallbackTarget`\n(`model` + `provider` + `endpoint`) and builds each entry through the same provider path as the\nprimary. Dropping it would defeat the main reason to configure a fallback (a local\nOpenAI-compatible proxy backing a hosted primary).\n\n**2 + 3. Lifecycle is SPLIT, and the split is the point.** VERIFIED: `FallbackModel.__aenter__`\n(fallback.py:167) enters every sub-model through an `AsyncExitStack` and `__aexit__` closes them —\nits own docstring says \"Enter all sub-models so their providers can manage HTTP client lifecycle\".\nSo clients created by pydantic-ai's OWN providers are pydantic-ai's to close, and the `FallbackModel`\nMUST be used as an async context manager or those sub-providers are never entered at all.\n\nThat does NOT cover rebar-created transports. `_build_retrying_anthropic_model` constructs an\n`httpx.AsyncClient` that REBAR owns and passes in; those still register on\n`ProviderSession._closeables` (`providers.py:102`, appended at `:214`/`:276`, drained at `:289`), one\nper Anthropic-typed entry. Rule: whoever CREATES the client closes it. Registering pydantic-ai's own\nclients on `_closeables` would double-close; omitting rebar's would leak one per call.\n\n**4. Capabilities are the conservative intersection, computed BEFORE the wrapper exists.** VERIFIED:\n`FallbackModel` has no `.profile`; its `.provider` property returns `None` (fallback.py:191) and\n`.base_url` returns `models[0].base_url`. So calling `capabilities_for()` on the wrapper — as\n`runner.py:365` does today for a single model — would silently yield primary-flavoured or default\ncapabilities rather than failing. Therefore capabilities, cache settings and tool-capability guards\nare computed from the RESOLVED CANDIDATE LIST (primary + every fallback) before the wrapper is built,\nand a capability holds only if EVERY candidate has it. `capabilities_for` is never called on a\n`FallbackModel`. Concretely: a chain containing Bedrock `claude-opus-4-8` (MEASURED\n`supports_temperature: False`) withdraws `temperature` for the whole chain — otherwise the run\nsucceeds or raises depending on which candidate answered, a failure reproducing only under provider\ndegradation.\n\n**5. `ran_model` comes from the RESPONSE, not from the model object.** VERIFIED:\n`FallbackModel.model_name` (fallback.py:194) returns the synthetic combined string\n`f\"fallback:{...}\"` and is USELESS for attestation. But `ModelResponse.model_name`\n(`messages.py:2106`) carries the name of the model that actually produced the response. So\n`provider_provenance` records the ordered `candidates` (known at build time) and `ran_model` read\nfrom the returned `ModelResponse`. A verdict recording only the primary would ATTEST THE WRONG MODEL\nwhenever a fallback fired — the misattribution 343b exists to prevent, which this feature must not\nreintroduce.\n\n**6. `fallback_on` is a CALLABLE PREDICATE over the EXISTING retryable set.** Two errors in the\nprevious revision, both caught: (a) \"derive from `ResolutionClass`\" while also naming exception TYPES\nwas a category error — `_map()` classifies an INSTANCE into a class, it does not yield a tuple of\ntypes; (b) the trigger set omitted `RETRY_NOW`, which is where `failure.py:259` maps\n`httpx.ConnectError` / `httpx.ConnectTimeout` — i.e. A DOWN LOCAL ENDPOINT, the exact scenario this\nticket's opening sentence motivates. The chain would not have fired for its own primary use case.\n\nVERIFIED resolution: `fallback_on` accepts a callable (fallback.py:92-107). rebar passes a predicate:\n\n    fallback_on = lambda exc: classify(exc) in _RETRYABLE or classify(exc) is CHANGE_PROVIDER_OR_MODEL\n\n`_RETRYABLE` is the EXISTING frozenset at `failure.py:51` — `{WAIT_AND_RETRY, RETRY_NOW}` — not a new\nlist. That is what makes the no-drift claim true: a future taxonomy change updates the fallback\nbehaviour automatically, whereas any hand-maintained set (including the one this plan carried until\nnow) drifts silently.\n\nWhat that covers, traced through `failure.py` rather than asserted:\n\n| class | reached by | falls back |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `RETRY_NOW` | `httpx.ConnectError`, `ConnectTimeout` (:259) — a down endpoint | YES |\n| `WAIT_AND_RETRY` | 429 rate-limit, 529, 5xx (:215-220); Read/Pool/Write timeouts (:261) | YES |\n| `CHANGE_PROVIDER_OR_MODEL` | `FallbackExceptionGroup` (:272), model-unavailable | YES |\n| `CHANGE_SETTINGS` | 401/403 (:223), `UserError` | no |\n| `CHANGE_INPUT` | 400 context-length, 413 (:225-228) | no |\n| `INCREASE_PROVIDER_LIMITS` | 402, and 429 WITH `insufficient_quota` (:216-217) | no |\n| `FIX_AGENT_DESIGN`, `NEEDS_INVESTIGATION` | agent/schema defects | no |\n\nNote the 429 SPLIT, which the review surfaced and a naive \"429 -> fall back\" rule gets wrong: a plain\nrate-limit 429 is `WAIT_AND_RETRY` and DOES fall back, but a 429 whose body carries\n`insufficient_quota` is `INCREASE_PROVIDER_LIMITS` and does NOT. Rationale for excluding it: quota or\npayment exhaustion is a SPEND problem, and silently relocating spend to another provider is worse\nthan failing loudly. This is a deliberate, arguable call — recorded here so it is revisited on\npurpose rather than discovered by surprise.\n\nExcluded for the same fail-loudly reason: `CHANGE_SETTINGS` (a credential problem must not be masked\nby shopping for a provider whose key happens to work) and `CHANGE_INPUT` (another provider will not\nfix an oversized request — the SIZE LADDER owns that path, and falling back would hide it).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] a test asserts a class without `fallback` builds an unwrapped model (no behaviour change)\n- [ ] a test asserts a class with `fallback` builds a FallbackModel whose default is the primary entry\n- [ ] a test asserts an error in the configured condition selects the fallback entry\n- [ ] a test asserts an error outside the condition PROPAGATES rather than failing over\n- [ ] the chosen `fallback_on` condition is documented in-code with the SDK-retry caveat\n\n- [ ] a test asserts a fallback entry carrying `endpoint` produces a model object built against THAT\n      endpoint — not one silently built against the provider default\n- [ ] a test asserts every fallback client is registered on the SAME `ProviderSession` and is closed\n      on teardown: with N Anthropic-typed fallbacks, `_closeables` holds N+1 clients and all are\n      closed exactly once (this fails against a per-entry-session implementation, which leaks)\n- [ ] a test asserts a chain containing a model with `supports_temperature: False` withdraws\n      `temperature` for the WHOLE chain, not just when that model is the primary\n- [ ] a test asserts `provider_provenance` records the ordered `candidates` AND a `ran_model` equal\n      to the model that actually answered — so a verdict produced by a fallback does not attest the\n      primary\n- [ ] a test asserts `fallback_on` fires on a 429/529/5xx and on a transport error, and does NOT\n      fire on 401/403 or on a 400 context-length error (the loud-failure cases)\n- [ ] a test asserts the `fallback_on` predicate is derived from `failure.py`'s `_RETRYABLE`\n      frozenset plus `CHANGE_PROVIDER_OR_MODEL` — NOT a hand-maintained tuple of exception types\n- [ ] a test asserts the predicate returns True for `httpx.ConnectError` (RETRY_NOW — the down-local-\n      endpoint case this ticket exists for), True for a 529/5xx, and False for 401/403 and for a 400\n      context-length error\n- [ ] a test asserts the 429 SPLIT: a plain rate-limit 429 falls back, a 429 carrying\n      `insufficient_quota` does NOT (it classifies as INCREASE_PROVIDER_LIMITS)\n- [ ] a test asserts `capabilities_for` is NEVER called with a `FallbackModel` instance (it has no\n      `.profile` and its `.provider` is None, so it would silently return default capabilities)\n- [ ] a test asserts `ran_model` is read from the returned `ModelResponse.model_name`, NOT from\n      `FallbackModel.model_name` (which is the synthetic `fallback:a,b` string)\n- [ ] a test asserts the `FallbackModel` is used as an async context manager, so its sub-models'\n      providers are entered and exited (pydantic-ai closes ITS clients; rebar-created retrying\n      transports remain on `ProviderSession._closeables`, closed exactly once, never double-closed)\n\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785503822736349001,"uuid":"d1084682-3755-4cb1-9cc7-3cf6b0389ebe"}